/wiki/Geometry_and_the_Imagination)\nciteturn825search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.3 years ago; Geometry and the Imagination is the English translation of the 1932 book Anschauliche Geometrie by David Hilbert and Stefan Cohn-Vossen. ... AMS Chelsea Pub.1999.\nGeometry and the Imagination\n1932 book by David Hilbert and Stefan Cohn-Vossen\n\nName: Geometry and the Imagination\nTitle Orig: Anschauliche Geometrie\nTranslator: Paul Neményi\nPublisher: Chelsea Publishing (American Mathematical Society)\nPub Date: 1952\nPages: 357\nIsbn: 9780821819982\nOclc: 542459\n\nGeometry and the Imagination is the English translation of the 1932 book Anschauliche Geometrie by David Hilbert and Stefan Cohn-Vossen.\n\nThe book was based on a series of lectures Hilbert made in the winter of 1920–21. The book is an attempt to present some then-current mathematical thought to \"contribute to a more just appreciation of mathematics by a wider range of people than just the specialists.\" It differentiates between two tendencies in mathematics and any other scientific research: on the one hand, toward abstraction and logical relations, correlating the subject matter in a systematic and orderly manner, and on the other hand an intuitive approach, which moves toward a more immediate grasp of and a \"live rapport\" with the same material. Further he asserts that intuitive understanding actually plays a major role for the researcher as well as anyone who wishes to study and appreciate Geometry.\n\n- David Hilbert, Stefan Cohn-Vossen. Geometry and the imagination. AMS Chelsea Pub. 1999.\n- Hilbert, page iv\n- Hilbert, page iii\n\n## Contents\n\nTopics covered by the chapters in the book include the Leibniz formula for , configurations of points and lines with equally many points on each line and equally many lines through each point,\ncurvature and non-Euclidean geometry, mechanical linkages, the classification of manifolds by their Euler characteristic, and the four color theorem.\n\n## Response\n\nThe Mathematical Association of America said about the book, \"this book is a masterpiece — a delightful classic that should never go out of print\". Physics Today called it \"a readable exposition of modern geometry and its relation to other branches of mathematics\". The Scientific Monthly said about it \"has been a classic for twenty years . . . Although it deals with elementary topics, it reaches the fringe of our knowledge in many directions\".\n\n- Geometry and the Imagination Mathematical Association of America.\n- Hilbert, D. Geometry and the Imagination. Physics Today. May 1953.\n- Coxeter, H.S.M. Review: Intuitive Geometry. The Scientific Monthly. February 1953.\n\n## References\n\n## External links\n\n* Geometry and the Imagination (https://archive.org/details/geometryimaginat00davi_0) at the Internet Archive\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[17] Hilbert, D. & Cohn-Vossen, S. 1932 *Geometry and the Imagination* American Mathematical Society; Reprint edition, 1999. (https://nara-wu.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/2002185/files/interface-focus_v2n5_pp575-581.pdf)\nciteturn825search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.9 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; 1932 *Geometry and the Imagination* American Mathematical Society; Reprint edition, 1999.\n[17] Hilbert, D. & Cohn-Vossen, S. 1932 *Geometry and the Imagination* American Mathematical Society; Reprint edition, 1999.\n\n[18] Charvolin, J. & Sadoc, J. F. 1987 Periodic systems of frustrated fluid films and bicontinuous cubic structures in liquid crystals *J. Physique* **48**, 1559-1569.\n\n[19] Mackay, A. L. & Terrones, H. 1991 Diamond from graphite *Nature* **352**, 762.\n\n[20] Nesper, R. & Leoni, S. 2001 On tilings and patterns on hyperbolic surfaces and their relation to structural chemistry *ChemPhysChem* **2**, 413-422.\n\n[21] Ramsden, S. J. Robins, V. & Hyde, S. T. 2009 Three-dimensional Euclidean nets from two-dimensional hyperbolic tilings: kaleidoscopic 